        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ancestral Puebloans</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Colorado Plateau around the Four Corners of the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, Colorado and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> meet; later also the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> valley and the Zuni, Acoma and Hopi country</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500 BCE–1600 CE: from the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farmers of the Basketmaker II period, whose beginning is debated, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a> (c. 850–1150) and the cliff dwellings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> (c. 1190–1285) to the founding of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> in 1598; their descendants are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> of today</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Cliff dwellings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace">Cliff Palace</a>; the great houses and roads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a> the largest; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiva">kivas</a>; black-on-white painted pottery and fine basketry; turquoise work; farming in a dry land</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations and surveys at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a>, Aztec, Canyon de Chelly, Betatakin and Keet Seel, Bandelier and Pecos; tree-ring dating, which fixes many buildings to the year; rock art and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiva">kiva</a> murals; the chronicles of the Coronado expedition; the oral traditions of the Hopi, Zuni and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a></span></div>
