    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Ancestral Puebloans farmed the dry Colorado Plateau for some three thousand years, moving from caves and pithouses to villages of joined stone and adobe rooms called pueblos.</li><li>Between about 850 and 1150 they built the great houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a>, linked by roads to dozens of outlying communities, and in the 13th century the cliff dwellings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a>.</li><li>Drought and conflict led them to leave the Four Corners by about 1300 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, Zuni, Acoma and Hopi country, where their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a>, still live.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many Ancestral Puebloan buildings can be dated to the year their roof beams were felled: in 1929 a charred beam found at Show Low, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, closed the gap in A. E. Douglass’s tree-ring record and gave the ruins exact dates.</p></aside>
    </section>

