    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a> farming begins on the Colorado Plateau in the Basketmaker II period</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 500</span><span class="rf-text">Basketmaker III villages take up pottery, the bow and arrow and beans</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 850–1150</span><span class="rf-text">The great houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a> among them, are built and linked by roads</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1130–1150</span><span class="rf-text">Drought; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco</a>’s power fades and Aztec on the Animas River becomes a leading centre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1190–1285</span><span class="rf-text">The cliff dwellings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace">Cliff Palace</a> among them, are built and lived in</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1276–1299</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Drought; by about 1300 the Four Corners region is empty as its people move south and east</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1540</span><span class="rf-text">The Coronado expedition storms the Zuni town of Hawikuh and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> pueblos</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1598</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> founds the Spanish colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ancestral Puebloans are the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>: the Hopi, the Zuni, the people of Acoma and Laguna and the pueblos of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>. Archaeologists long called them the Anasazi, a word from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> language usually translated as “enemy ancestors” or “ancient enemies”. Many Pueblo people consider the name derogatory and object to it, and since the late 20th century archaeologists and the National Park Service have generally used “Ancestral Puebloans” or “Ancestral Pueblo peoples” instead; the Hopi call them Hisatsinom, “ancient people”.</p><p>Their history is usually told in the stages of the Pecos Classification, agreed at a meeting of archaeologists called by Alfred V. Kidder at Pecos, New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in 1927: a sequence of Basketmaker and Pueblo periods, later fixed to calendar years by tree-ring dating. They lived across the Colorado Plateau around the Four Corners, where Arizona, New Mexico, Colorado and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> meet, and archaeologists distinguish regional traditions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco</a> in north-western New Mexico, the Northern San Juan or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> region of south-western Colorado and south-eastern Utah, and Kayenta in north-eastern Arizona.</p><p>The first farmers of the plateau are called Basketmakers, after the fine coiled and twined baskets found with their burials in dry caves and rock shelters. Descendants of the Archaic hunters and gatherers of the region, they took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, which had spread north from Mexico, and squash during the Basketmaker II period, which some archaeologists begin as early as about 1500 BCE, though the date varies from place to place and is debated. They hunted with the spear-thrower, or atlatl, wore sandals woven from yucca fibre and blankets of rabbit fur, and from the first centuries CE lived in shallow pithouses and stored their harvests in pits and stone-lined cists.</p><p>In the Basketmaker III period, from about 500 CE, pottery came into general use, the bow and arrow replaced the atlatl and beans joined the crops, and people settled in villages of deeper pithouses, some with large communal structures. From about 750, in the Pueblo I period, they began to build rows of joined rooms above ground, first of poles and mud and later of stone, while the old pithouse lived on as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiva">kiva</a>, a round, partly underground chamber for ritual and meetings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito.jpg" alt="Pueblo Bonito" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pueblo_Bonito_Cliff_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Q. Jacobs, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancestral Puebloan life rested on farming in a dry and unpredictable land, at heights where frost could cut short the growing season. Families grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> where it was warm enough; they caught rain and runoff with check dams, terraces, ditches and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, and planted fields in many places to spread the risk of drought or frost. Turkeys were kept, at first mainly for their feathers and later for meat, and hunting deer and rabbits and gathering piñon nuts and wild plants added to the diet. Grinding maize on stone metates took hours every day, work done mainly by women, as it was among later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a>.</p><p>A household lived in a suite of rooms in a pueblo, a block of joined rooms of stone or adobe, often of several storeys and entered by ladders through hatches in the roofs, and shared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiva">kiva</a> and plaza with its neighbours. Among the Hopi, Zuni and Acoma of later times descent and the ownership of houses passed through the female line, and many archaeologists think this was already so among their ancestors. Communities grew and shrank as people moved on when soil, wood or water ran short, a habit of movement that Pueblo traditions remember in the migrations of the clans. Early in the 13th century some 20,000 people lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> region alone.</p><p>Between about 850 and 1150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> became the centre of a world that reached across the San Juan Basin. Its builders raised about fifteen great houses, planned buildings of several storeys; the largest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a>, built in stages over three centuries, had some 800 rooms rising four and five storeys around a D-shaped plaza. Straight roads, some running for tens of kilometres, linked the canyon with dozens of outlying great houses. How many people lived in the canyon, and whether Chaco was ruled by an elite or was mainly a centre of pilgrimage and feasting, are much debated. In one room of Pueblo Bonito, the richest burial place known in the South-West, most of the people buried over about 330 years with thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> of turquoise and shell belonged, a genetic study published in 2017 found, to a single family line related through their mothers, which suggests a hereditary elite. A long drought that began about 1130 accompanied the unravelling of the Chacoan world after 1140, while Aztec, on the Animas River to the north, became a leading centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kiva"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiva.jpg" alt="Kiva" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kiva">Kiva</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandelier_Kiva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancestral Puebloan religion is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiva">kivas</a> and shrines, from objects and rock art, and from the religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> today, who see the old villages not as abandoned ruins but as places where their ancestors still dwell. Living tradition is a guide to the past, though not a certain one.</p><p>The kiva was the heart of ritual life. Developed from the pithouse, it was entered by a ladder through the roof and had a hearth, a ventilator, a bench around the walls and, in the floor, a small hole called the sipapu, which later Pueblo belief connects with the place where people emerged into this world from the worlds below. Great kivas, some nearly 20 metres across, served whole communities; Casa Rinconada at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco</a> is among the largest.</p><p>The builders watched the sky. Several great houses at Chaco are aligned on the cardinal directions, and some scholars argue that others were aligned on the rising and setting of the sun and moon at key moments of the year; on Fajada Butte a spiral carved in the rock, the “Sun Dagger” recorded by Anna Sofaer in 1977, is pierced by blades of sunlight at the solstices and equinoxes. Ritual at Chaco used goods from far away: cylinder jars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a> held drinks made with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, identified from residues in 2009, and scarlet macaws, tropical parrots prized for their feathers, were brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>.</p><p>After 1300 a new religious tradition spread among the Pueblos. Masked figures like the katsinas of Hopi and Zuni religion, spirit beings associated with rain who are represented by masked dancers in the plazas, appear in rock art and in murals painted on kiva walls in the 14th and 15th centuries, at Awat’ovi in the Hopi country and Kuaua on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> among other places. Many archaeologists think that the katsina tradition, with its ceremonies for the whole town, helped to bind together the large new communities formed by migrants from different places. Pueblo ceremonies remain private, and much of their meaning is not shared with outsiders, a choice that scholars today respect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cliff-palace.jpg" alt="Cliff Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace">Cliff Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cliff_Palace-Colorado-Mesa_Verde_NP.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tobi 87, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancestral Puebloan builders worked in stone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and adobe without metal tools, draught animals or wheels. Chacoan masons built thick walls with a rubble core faced with finely laid sandstone in banded patterns, usually hidden under plaster, and roofed their rooms with timbers: an estimated 200,000 trees, many from the Chuska Mountains more than 80 kilometres to the west, were carried to the canyon on foot. From the end of the 12th century many families of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> region moved from the mesa tops into villages fitted into natural alcoves in the canyon walls, with rooms, towers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiva">kivas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace">Cliff Palace</a>, the largest, has about 150 rooms and 23 kivas and housed perhaps 100 to 120 people; Betatakin and Keet Seel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and the White House in Canyon de Chelly are other cliff villages of the 13th century.</p><p>Potters built their vessels from coils of clay, without the wheel. Grey cooking jars were later finished with corrugated surfaces, the coils left pinched on the outside, while bowls, jars and mugs for serving and storage were painted in black on a white slip with geometric designs whose regional and changing styles allow archaeologists to date and place them. After 1300 potters on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> painted with glazes, and those of the Hopi country made yellow polychrome wares such as the Sikyatki style, which the Hopi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewa">Tewa</a> potter Nampeyo (c. 1859–1942) revived from ancient pottery found at Sikyatki and other old villages.</p><p>Weavers made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth and warm blankets of turkey feathers wrapped around cords of yucca, and jewellers made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and mosaics of turquoise, jet and shell. Rock art, pecked and painted on canyon walls, ranges from the broad-shouldered human figures of the Basketmakers to the masked figures of later centuries.</p><p>The Ancestral Puebloans are among the most precisely dated ancient peoples of the world, thanks to their timbers. The astronomer A. E. Douglass developed tree-ring dating early in the 20th century, and once his sequence was complete in 1929 it showed, for example, that Aztec was built between 1111 and 1120 and the great cliff dwellings in the 13th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon.jpg" alt="Chaco Canyon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chaco_Canyon_Chetro_Ketl_great_kiva_plaza_NPS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Park Service (United States), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ancestral Puebloans traded widely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco</a> drew turquoise from several sources, among them the Cerrillos Hills near Santa Fe, and worked it by the thousand into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and mosaics; shells came from the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and the Pacific, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> bells from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and macaws and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Pottery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, salt and stone tools moved between the communities of the plateau.</p><p>They had varied neighbours. To the south lived the Hohokam of the Sonoran Desert, who watered their fields from long canals, and the Mogollon of the mountains, whose Mimbres branch painted famous black-on-white bowls; to the north lived the Fremont of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>. By about 1500 Athapaskan-speaking peoples, the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>, had entered the region. The town of Paquimé, or Casas Grandes, in Chihuahua, which flourished from about 1200 to 1450 and bred macaws, linked the South-West with Mexico, and in the 15th and 16th centuries Pecos and other eastern pueblos traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, cotton cloth and pottery to Plains peoples for bison meat and hides.</p><p>Relations among the Ancestral Puebloan communities themselves were not always peaceful. Around 1150 and again in the later 13th century villages moved behind walls and towers or into alcoves that were hard to reach, and skeletons from several sites show people killed in attacks. At a few sites butchered and burned human bones have led some archaeologists to argue for cannibalism, an interpretation that is fiercely debated and that many Pueblo people reject.</p><p>The Spanish arrived in 1540, when the expedition of Francisco Vázquez de Coronado, searching for the fabled Seven Cities of Cíbola, stormed the Zuni town of Hawikuh. Coronado’s men went on to the Hopi villages, Acoma and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, where their demands for food, clothing and shelter in the winter of 1540–1541 led to the Tiguex War, in which they destroyed Tiwa pueblos. In 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> led settlers, soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> north and founded the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>; after Acoma’s warriors killed a party of his soldiers, his forces stormed the mesa in January 1599 and killed several hundred of its people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo.jpg" alt="Acoma Pueblo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo">Acoma Pueblo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acoma_Pueblo_Sky_City_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scott Catron, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By about 1300 the Ancestral Puebloans had left the Four Corners. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a> the last inhabitants had gone by about 1285. Tree rings record a severe drought from 1276 to 1299, the Great Drought, but the causes were many: worn-out soils and forests, cold, rising violence and perhaps the pull of new ways of life in the south. People moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> valley and its tributaries, to the Little Colorado and to the Zuni, Acoma and Hopi country, where they joined or founded large towns built around plazas. They did not vanish: Pueblo traditions describe the migrations of their clans from the old homes, and several Rio Grande pueblos trace their ancestry to Mesa Verde, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco</a> and other sites.</p><p>Under Spanish rule the Pueblos endured tribute, forced labour and the suppression of their religion, and on 10 August 1680 they rose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope">Popé</a>, killing about 400 colonists and 21 Franciscan friars and driving the Spanish from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> reconquered it from 1692. Acoma, Taos and the Hopi village of Oraibi are among the oldest continuously inhabited settlements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>United States soldiers surveyed Chaco in 1849, and in December 1888 the ranchers Richard Wetherill and Charlie Mason came upon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cliff-palace">Cliff Palace</a> while looking for stray <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Digging for collectors followed: the Swedish scholar Gustaf Nordenskiöld removed a collection from Mesa Verde in 1891 that ended in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and Wetherill and George H. Pepper excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-bonito">Pueblo Bonito</a> for the American Museum of Natural History from 1896 to 1900. Concern at such losses helped to bring about the Antiquities Act of 1906, the year Mesa Verde National Park was created. Mesa Verde, Chaco Culture and Taos Pueblo are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p>The romantic image of a mysterious people who vanished has given way to a history of migration and continuity, increasingly written together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a> themselves, and the name Ancestral Puebloans has largely replaced the old one.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ancestral Puebloans farmed the dry Colorado Plateau around the Four Corners for some three thousand years, building the great houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-canyon">Chaco Canyon</a> and the cliff dwellings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-verde">Mesa Verde</a>. Around 1300 they left the region for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, Zuni, Acoma and Hopi country, and their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a>, keep their languages, ceremonies and villages alive today.</em></p>

